{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1782357591351,"sessionID":"ses_103385283ffe30G1XfRK45Z652","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"functions.bash:11","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"python3 --version && python3 -m pytest --version","description":"Check Python and pytest availability"},"output":"Python 3.12.12\npytest 8.2.2\n","metadata":{"output":"Python 3.12.12\npytest 8.2.2\n","exit":0,"description":"Check Python and pytest availability","truncated":false},"title":"Check Python and pytest availability","time":{"start":1782357591330,"end":1782357591349}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"               Excellent. I now understand the data structure. Notice:\n\n- `Brasileirao_Matches.csv`: datetime, home_team (with state suffix), home_team_state, away_team, away_team_state, home_goal, away_goal, season, round\n- `Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv`: round, datetime, home_team (may include suffix/state in name), away_team, home_goal, away_goal, season\n- `Libertadores_Matches.csv`: datetime, home_team, away_team, home_goal, away_goal, season, stage (home_goal/away_goal are int not float here)\n- `BR-Football-Dataset.csv`: tournament, home, home_goal, away_goal, away, home_corner, away_corner, home_attack, away_attack, home_shots, away_shots, time, date, ht_diff, at_diff, ht_result, at_result, total_corners\n- `novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv`: ID, Data (DD/MM/YYYY), Ano, Rodada, Equipe_mandante, Equipe_visitante, Gols_mandante, Gols_visitante, Mandante_UF, Visitante_UF, Vencedor, Arena, OBS\n- `fifa_data.csv`: many columns; key Name, Age, Nationality, Overall, Potential, Club, Position, Jersey Number, Height, Weight plus skill ratings.\n\nI need to handle team name variations (e.g., Palmeiras vs Palmeiras-SP) and dates.\n\nApproach to avoid heavy dependencies:\n- Use standard library + pandas? Pandas is likely available but could be heavy for requirements. The task says \"Write all code files to the current directory\" and \"Make sure the code builds and tests pass.\" Since it's a Python project, I can require `pandas` if needed for tests. But minimal dependencies might use stdlib `csv`. However pandas greatly simplifies data handling. Let me check if pandas is installed.\n\nActually, since the user only told me to implement and test, I can choose dependencies. I'll use `mcp` Python SDK for the server. It depends on pydantic and anyio. I'll use a virtual environment and install required packages. Better to write a `pyproject.toml` with dependencies.\n\nBut in benchmark environments, internet may not be available to install packages. I should check if `mcp` is available. If not, I can implement a simple stdio MCP server from scratch using JSON-RPC. That avoids dependency on `mcp` package and only needs standard library plus maybe pydantic (but can avoid pydantic too). Given uncertainty, I think implementing a minimal MCP server manually is safer: parse JSON-RPC lines from stdin, respond to `initialize`, `tools/list`, `tools/call`. But the spec also says MCP server, so using the official SDK might be expected. However the success criteria don't require official SDK; they require functional behavior.\n\nGiven I should make tests pass, and the environment may not have `mcp` installed, I'll implement a lightweight stdio MCP server manually with no external dependencies besides maybe pandas. But tests can avoid pandas too if I write my data loader with stdlib csv. Since the CSVs are large and need parsing, stdlib csv is sufficient.\n\nLet me design a clean architecture:\n\nProject files:\n- `pyproject.toml` (or `requirements.txt`) - dependencies: none beyond stdlib; pytest for testing with dev option.\n- `soccer_mcp/__init__.py`\n- `soccer_mcp/data_loader.py` - loads and normalizes all CSVs into a unified structure\n- `soccer_mcp/normalize.py` - team name normalization, date parsing\n- `soccer_mcp/queries.py` - query engine for matches/teams/players/competitions/stats\n- `soccer_mcp/server.py` - stdio MCP server\n- `tests/test_queries.py` - pytest tests (BDD-style comments)\n- `README.md` update maybe no need to update per prompt but user said implement TASK, not update README.\n- `main.py` maybe entry point.\n\nThe prompts.txt says \"#ignore this file\". So I will ignore it.\n\nImportant requirement from TASK: \"Write all code files to the current directory.\" Does that mean no subdirectories? It says \"current directory\", but also conventional Python projects use subdirs. Maybe they mean within workspace root. I can create `src/` or package directory. To be safe, maybe keep top-level modules and a `tests/` directory. That's common. The README original mentions maybe update README; but user didn't request updating; still maybe worth a brief update. The first instruction says \"Read TASK.md in the current directory and implement everything it asks for.\" It asks to create an MCP server, not necessarily update README beyond code. But original prompt suggests update README. User here didn't ask; I'll focus on code and tests, maybe only update README if necessary.\n\nGiven \"Write all code files to the current directory\" I'll create top-level package `brazilian_soccer_mcp/` or modules. That counts as current directory (project root). Tests in `tests/`.\n\nNow, design details:\n\nData model:\n- Unified match record: `match_id`? Could derive from source+index. Fields: competition, season, round/stage (optional), date (ISO string maybe datetime.date), home_team (normalized), away_team (normalized), home_original, away_original, home_goal, away_goal, plus extended stats.\n\nData loader:\n- For each CSV, read all rows, parse dates, normalize team names, infer competition. Build a list of dicts.\n- For `Brasileirao_Matches.csv`: competition 'Brasileirão', season from 'season', round from 'round'. Date parse 'datetime'.\n- For `Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv`: competition 'Copa do Brasil', round from 'round', season from 'season', date from 'datetime'.\n- For `Libertadores_Matches.csv`: competition 'Copa Libertadores', season from 'season', stage from 'stage', date from 'datetime'.\n- For `BR-Football-Dataset.csv`: competition from 'tournament' (can be 'Copa do Brasil', 'Brasileirão', etc.), date parse from 'date', no round maybe.\n- For `novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv`: competition 'Brasileirão', season from 'Ano', round from 'Rodada', date parse 'Data' DD/MM/YYYY.\n- Player data from `fifa_data.csv`: list of dicts with cleaned names.\n\nTeam name normalization:\n- Strip state suffix like '-SP', '-RJ', '-MG', '-PR', etc.\n- Handle parentheses? e.g., 'Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ' etc. We want base name maybe \"Boavista\". We can remove things after first '(' and trim, remove state suffix. Also remove leading/trailing spaces.\n- Full names in historical data not much; e.g., 'Athletico-PR', 'Grêmio'. Need map common variants: \"Atlético Mineiro\" vs \"Atlético-MG\", \"Athletico Paranaense\" vs \"Athletico-PR\" vs \"Atlético-PR\". Data uses \"Athletico-PR\" and \"Atlético-MG\". We can normalize suffix stripping and common synonyms.\n- Use unidecode? Not necessary but can do accent-insensitive. Better: normalize by lowercasing, removing accents, removing words like 'fc', 'ec', etc. For matching, we can implement a matching function: canonicalize name (lowercase, remove accents, remove punctuation, remove common suffixes/stopwords) and compare. Also support alias map for common variants: \"Corinthians\", \"São Paulo\" vs \"Sao Paulo\", \"Vasco\" vs \"Vasco da Gama\", etc.\n\nGiven complexity, a robust but simple approach:\n- Define `normalize_team_name(name)` returns a tuple: display_name, canonical_key.\n- Strip state suffix after dash with 2-letter uppercase.\n- Remove parenthetical content.\n- Strip leading \"Clube\", \"Sport Club\", \"Esporte Clube\", \"Futebol Clube\" etc. Maybe not important.\n- Canonical key: lowercase, remove accents using `unicodedata.normalize('NFKD').encode('ascii','ignore').decode('ascii')`, remove non-alphanumeric, then remove stopwords: 'fc','ec','sc','cep','clube','sport','esporte','futebol','para','de','do','da'. Also remove state abbreviations.\n- Then matching by canonical key containment or equality: If query canonical key equals full key or is a substring? For \"Flamengo\" vs \"Flamengo-RJ\", query key 'flamengo', database key 'flamengo' -> match. For \"São Paulo\" vs \"Sao Paulo\" accents removed -> match. For \"Sao Paulo FC\" -> key 'saopaulo' after stopwords, database 'saopaulo' -> match. For \"Corinthians\" and \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" -> both keys 'corinthians' (stopwords removed). Good.\n- Implement a helper `find_matching_teams(name, all_canonical)` returns list of canonical keys whose name matches. For queries with a team name, we match teams whose canonical key equals query key or contains it as a complete word maybe.\n\nPlayer queries:\n- Filter player list by name substring (case-insensitive, accent-insensitive). Nationality: \"Brazil\" maybe they use \"Brazil\"; we can normalize compare.\n- Club: filter by club name matching similar normalization (e.g., Flamengo).\n- Position filter: exact or canonical.\n\nStats:\n- Win/loss/draw counts per team match list. For team matches, treat as a list of matches where team is home or away. Use normalized name canonical.\n- Calculate home vs away record, goals for/against.\n- Head-to-head: for two teams, filter matches where both participate.\n- Compute standings from matches by group (competition+season): points 3/1/0, goal diff, wins, etc. Sort.\n- Relegation: bottom 4 in Brasileirão based on final standings. We can compute standings and return last 4.\n- Biggest wins: sort by goal difference. Use absolute diff.\n- Average goals per match: mean of home_goal+away_goal.\n\nMCP tools:\nWe need to expose tools that an LLM can call with structured parameters. Define ~8-10 tools:\n- `find_matches(team1, team2=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, limit=20)`\n- `team_statistics(team, season=None, competition=None)`\n- `head_to_head(team1, team2)`\n- `find_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, limit=20)`\n- `top_players(nationality=None, club=None, position=None, limit=10)`\n- `competition_standings(competition, season)`\n- `relegated_teams(season)`\n- `biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)`\n- `average_goals(competition=None, season=None)`\n- `team_competitions(team)` maybe.\n- `get_player(name)` etc.\n\nIn the server, we accept these tool names and JSON params. Return text format.\n\nTesting with pytest:\nWe can write tests not requiring MCP transport, testing query functions directly. Use BDD style in docstrings or via pytest-bdd (need package). To avoid dependency, I'll write plain pytest tests with comments indicating Given/When/Then. That satisfies \"BDD GWT structured PyTest\" from original prompt? The user didn't specifically require pytest-bdd; TASK.md says \"Use BDD (Behavior-Driven Development) test scenarios\". We can include Gherkin comments.\n\nWe need \"at least 20 sample questions can be answered\" - tests can cover many.\n\nImplement \"Maintain a detailed context block comment at the start of every code file\" - original prompt suggests block comment at start. I should add module-level docstrings/comments. And update README.md if original requested; but not necessarily. Since the user instruction is implement TASK, I might add a brief README update. However current README already describes data; maybe add usage section.\n\nLet me now check if pytest is installed, create virtual environment maybe. But the task says write code, tests pass. I'll create a virtual environment in project root to install test dependencies? Or use system python. Let me check python version and pytest availability.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_efcca8298001jZxZ50HiFjppmn","sessionID":"ses_103385283ffe30G1XfRK45Z652","messageID":"msg_efcc9953a0