{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1782357795167,"sessionID":"ses_103385283ffe30G1XfRK45Z652","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"functions.bash:15","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport csv, collections, os\nbase = '/private/var/folders/t6/p1v727892_qc9xqqdzq2rpym0000gn/T/retort-local-tjpsd7_l/retort-8ea0ffcc10d3/data/kaggle'\nfor fn in ['Brasileirao_Matches.csv','Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv']:\n    print('\\n', fn)\n    with open(os.path.join(base, fn), encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        rows = list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    rounds = collections.Counter(r['round'] for r in rows)\n    print(sorted(rounds.items(), key=lambda x: str(x[0]))[:20])\n    print('... total distinct', len(rounds))\nPY","description":"Inspect round values in match files"},"output":"\n Brasileirao_Matches.csv\n[('1', 110), ('10', 110), ('11', 110), ('12', 110), ('13', 110), ('14', 110), ('15', 110), ('16', 110), ('17', 110), ('18', 110), ('19', 110), ('2', 110), ('20', 110), ('21', 110), ('22', 110), ('23', 110), ('24', 110), ('25', 110), ('26', 110), ('27', 110)]\n... total distinct 38\n\n Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv\n[('1', 334), ('2', 376), ('3', 258), ('4', 140), ('5', 123), ('6', 62), ('7', 30), ('8', 14)]\n... total distinct 8\n","metadata":{"output":"\n Brasileirao_Matches.csv\n[('1', 110), ('10', 110), ('11', 110), ('12', 110), ('13', 110), ('14', 110), ('15', 110), ('16', 110), ('17', 110), ('18', 110), ('19', 110), ('2', 110), ('20', 110), ('21', 110), ('22', 110), ('23', 110), ('24', 110), ('25', 110), ('26', 110), ('27', 110)]\n... total distinct 38\n\n Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv\n[('1', 334), ('2', 376), ('3', 258), ('4', 140), ('5', 123), ('6', 62), ('7', 30), ('8', 14)]\n... total distinct 8\n","exit":0,"description":"Inspect round values in match files","truncated":false},"title":"Inspect round values in match files","time":{"start":1782357795164,"end":1782357795166}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"                 The official `mcp` Python SDK is installed in the environment. It provides `mcp.server.fastmcp.FastMCP` for easy stdio server. I should use it, because it's available and appropriate. It uses pydantic. This reduces manual JSON-RPC handling. Let's verify we can create FastMCP. The version likely has `FastMCP`. Use `mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP`.\n\nBut using external dependency means final code fails to run if not installed. However tests can import mcp because installed. To ensure \"builds and tests pass\" in this environment, it's fine. For portability, add `pyproject.toml` with `[project] dependencies = [\"mcp\"]` and dev dependencies pytest. The test command can install into a venv if needed.\n\nNote: The data loader should avoid pandas to reduce dependencies and potential performance? Pandas not necessarily necessary. But CSV files up to ~18k rows, stdlib csv is fine and fast.\n\nLet me design modules.\n\nPotential file structure:\n- `pyproject.toml`\n- `soccer_mcp/__init__.py` (empty, version)\n- `soccer_mcp/normalize.py`\n- `soccer_mcp/data_loader.py`\n- `soccer_mcp/engine.py` (query functions)\n- `soccer_mcp/server.py`\n- `tests/__init__.py`\n- `tests/test_engine.py`\n\nUse FastMCP as entry point.\n\nNeed to maintain a context block comment at start of every code file. I'll include docstrings with project context and module purpose.\n\nNow we need to implement name normalization robustly but not overly complex.\n\nNormalization plan:\n\nWe will load data and create for each match:\n- `source`: file name\n- `competition`: normalized competition name\n- `season`: int or float converted to int if possible, else None\n- `round`: string (from 'round' or 'Rodada')\n- `stage`: string\n- `date`: `datetime.date` object or None if unparseable\n- `datetime`: original string or iso string\n- `home_team_canonical`: canonical key\n- `away_team_canonical`: canonical key\n- `home_team_display`: somewhat standardized display name derived from original with state suffix stripped\n- `away_team_display`: same\n- `home_team_original`: raw name\n- `away_team_original`: raw name\n- `home_goal`: int or None\n- `away_goal`: int or None\n- `extended`: dict with extra stats (corners etc.) for BR-Football dataset\n\nFor competition normalization, map known values:\n- For BR-Football-Dataset `tournament` field to canonical names: if contains 'brasileir' -> 'Brasileirão', 'copa do brasil' -> 'Copa do Brasil', 'libertadores' -> 'Copa Libertadores', else keep as title.\n\nDate parsing:\n- Try `datetime.strptime` with multiple formats: `%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S`, `%Y-%m-%d`, `%d/%m/%Y`.\n- Also some dates may include timezone? Probably not.\n\nScore parsing:\n- Convert to int; handle NaN/empty -> None. Maybe pandas loaded them as floats with .0. We'll strip.\n\nTeam name normalization details:\n\n`normalize_team_name(name)`:\n- If not string, return None.\n- Remove leading/trailing whitespace.\n- Strip state suffix pattern ` - [A-Z]{2}$` and also `-[A-Z]{2}$` (e.g., Palmeiras-SP). Actually data has \"Palmeiras-SP\" no space. We want strip both.\n- Remove parenthetical content: `(...)` (including nested? simple).\n- Collapse whitespace.\n- `original_cleaned` is after suffix/parentheses removal.\n- Compute canonical:\n  - `base = unicodedata.normalize('NFKD', original_cleaned).encode('ascii','ignore').decode('ascii')`\n  - lowercase\n  - replace '&' with 'and'? maybe not needed.\n  - remove all non-alphanumeric (keep letters and digits).\n  - remove common words: stopwords = {'fc','ecf','sc','scfp','ce','ec','ac','aa','cam','amg','athletico','atletico'? Wait, removing 'atletico' can collapse Atlético Mineiro and Athletico Paranaense? We don't want to collapse different teams. So be cautious with stopwords. Better: Don't remove 'atletico' because Atlético-MG vs Athletico-PR differ only by state after normalization; canonical: 'atleticomg' vs 'athleticopr'. We can also add alias mapping: 'athletico' -> 'atletico' to treat Athletico Paranaense same as Atlético-PR? Actually team Athletico Paranaense rebranded from Atlético Paranaense; data uses 'Athletico-PR' and 'Atlético-PR' might both appear. It would be reasonable to alias 'athletico' to 'atletico'. But this would merge Athletico-PR with Atlético-PR only; if another team called Athletico? Not in Brazil. OK.\n  - Stopwords that are generic: 'esporte','clube','sport','futebol','goias' no. Remove 'de','do','da','e'. Should we keep 'gremio', 'corinthians' etc. yes.\n  - Also strip state abbreviations. But state abbreviations also part of DB key? Data includes '-SP', '-RJ'. We strip them, but if a team name contains 'SP' as separate word unintentionally? We'll remove 2-letter uppercase at end only, not within.\n- For matching queries, we can just canonicalize the query name and find teams whose canonical key contains query key or vice versa. For exact queries maybe better.\n\nCommon aliases map (applied before canonicalization):\n- Map full official names to common short names. But we only need to match queries. Could have a dictionary of aliases to canonical keys. E.g., \"corinthians\" maps to canonical of \"Corinthians\". Since our canonicalization of \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" yields 'corinthians', both query and db match. For \"SPFC\"? alias.\n- Map \"sao paulo\" -> canonical 'saopaulo'.\n- Map \"athletico paranaense\" -> 'atletico'.\n- Map \"atletico mineiro\" -> 'atletico' plus state mg - but canonical gives 'atleticomg' while Athletico-PR canonical gives 'atleticopr', so state preserved. Good. But query \"Atlético-MG\" and \"Atlético Mineiro\" both produce 'atleticomg'? Query 'atletico mineiro' canonical after stopwords: 'atleticomg' (since 'mineiro' not stopword). Good. Query 'Athletico' alone would match any atletico... Not ideal but common.\n- Map \"vasco\" / \"vasco da gama\" -> canonical 'vascodagama' (with stopwords remove 'da' -> 'vascogama'? Actually 'vasco da gama' -> ascii 'vasco da gama', lower, remove non-alphanum -> 'vascodagama', remove stopwords 'da' -> 'vascogama'. But raw 'Vasco' canonical -> 'vasco'. These won't match. Need alias for Vasco da Gama: remove 'da gama'? Better canonical should keep 'vasco' only? But then Vasco da Gama matches 'Vasco'. Also there might be \"Clube de Regatas do Flamengo\" full name -> after stopwords 'regatasflamengo' maybe not 'flamengo'. Hmm.\n- We can implement alias expansion: given a query, generate a set of possible canonical keys including after alias replacements.\n- Simpler: add a synonym map from query tokens to canonical, not over-engineer.\n- Define a small mapping dictionary of display aliases for well-known clubs:\n  - 'flamengo': 'Flamengo'\n  - 'corinthians': 'Corinthians'\n  - 'sao paulo': 'São Paulo'\n  - 'santos': 'Santos'\n  - 'palmeiras': 'Palmeiras'\n  - 'vasco': 'Vasco da Gama'\n  - 'vasco da gama': 'Vasco da Gama'\n  - 'gremio': 'Grêmio'\n  - 'internacional': 'Internacional'\n  - 'cruzeiro': 'Cruzeiro'\n  - 'atletico mineiro': 'Atlético Mineiro'\n  - 'atletico mg': 'Atlético Mineiro'\n  - 'athletico pr': 'Athletico Paranaense'\n  - 'athletico paranaense': 'Athletico Paranaense'\n  - 'atletico paranaense': 'Athletico Paranaense'\n  - 'fluminense': 'Fluminense'\n  - 'botafogo': 'Botafogo'\n  - 'bahia': 'Bahia'\n  - 'sport': 'Sport Recife' maybe but 'Sport' also could mean many; map to 'Sport Recife' maybe.\n  - 'fortaleza': 'Fortaleza'\n  - 'ceara': 'Ceará'\n  - 'coritiba': 'Coritiba'\n\nBut display mapping is less crucial for matching than canonical key matching.\n\nImplementation approach for matching:\n- `canonicalize(name)`: returns a tuple of normalized key and set of alternative keys.\n- For team records we store a canonical key generated from the cleaned name.\n- For search query, we canonicalize and generate alternative canonical forms using alias map. Then we compare to stored canonical keys.\n\nMaybe better: create a lookup dictionary mapping canonical key -> display name by scanning all teams after loading. Then query canonicalization only needs to find stored key equality. If not equal, try substring: all stored keys containing query key. That handles \"Flamengo\" query matches \"Clube de Regatas do Flamengo\"? But canonical of that full name might be 'regatasflamengo' so contains 'flamengo'. Good. \"Vasco\" query key 'vasco' will match stored 'vascodagama'? But canonical of 'Vasco da Gama' after stopwords maybe 'vascogama'? Need decide.\n\nLet's make canonicalization keep all letters/digits and remove only accents/punctuation; no stopwords. Then \"Vasco da Gama\" -> 'vascodagama', query \"Vasco\" -> 'vasco', substring match yes. That works for full names. But \"São Paulo FC\" -> 'saopaulofc', query \"Sao Paulo\" -> 'saopaulo', substring yes. \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" -> 'sportclubcorinthianspaulista', query \"Corinthians\" -> 'corinthians', substring yes. Good.\nBut substring can have false positives: e.g., query \"Sport\" matches \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" because they share 'sport'? And query \"Santos\" matches? Fine? Query \"Sport\" could be ambiguous. We can rank matches where query key at word boundaries, but list all.\n- For data, we also want to normalize \"Athletico-PR\" and \"Atlético-PR\" to same canonical key? Since canonical removes '-PR' and accents, both become 'athleticoparanaense'? Wait raw \"Athletico-PR\": strip suffix -> \"Athletico\"; remove parentheses none; remove accents -> \"Athletico\"; lower; remove non-alnum -> \"athletico\". Raw \"Atlético-PR\" -> \"atletico\". They differ. We could alias 'athletico' to 'atletico' before canonicalization to merge. But then \"Athletico-PR\" and \"Atlético-MG\" both become 'atletico...' but state suffix distinguishes. If we replace 'athletico' with 'atletico' and then strip suffix, we get 'atletico' for both, losing state! Need keep state. Better only apply alias after suffix stripping, replacing whole word 'athletico' with 'atletico', resulting 'atletico' for Athletico-PR and 'atletico' for Atlético-PR as base, but then if we don't strip state suffix, canonical includes state: base \"Atletico-PR\" -> 'atleticopr'; base \"Atletico-MG\" -> 'atleticomg'. So we need preserve state suffix when alias applied. That requires ordering: first extract state suffix, then canonicalize base (with alias), then append state code. Or simpler: canonical = canonicalize_base(name without state) + state_code. For \"Athletico-PR\", base=\"Athletico\", state=\"PR\", alias base to \"Atletico\", canonical=\"atleticopr\". For \"Atlético-MG\", canonical \"atleticomg\". Good.\n\nSo canonicalization process:\n1. Extract state suffix (with/without space) at end: regex `r\"\\s*-\\s*([A-Z]{2})$\"`, optional. Keep `state_code` uppercase.\n2. Remove parenthetical content `\\(.*?\\)`.\n3. Trim whitespace -> `base_name`.\n4. Apply accent removal, lower, remove non-alphanumeric to get `base_key`.\n5. Apply aliases to base_key: e.g., `athletico` -> `atletico`; maybe `spfc` -> `saopaulo`; `crf` -> `flamengo`; `timao` -> `corinthians`; `tricolor` ambiguous. Keep minimal.\n6. Canonical = base_key + (state_code if present).\n\nFor matching query:\n- If query includes state suffix, extract similarly. Else don't add state.\n- Generate `query_keys`: base_key, and if base_key in reverse alias map also other aliases? E.g., query \"atletico\" should match both \"Atletico-PR\" and \"Atletico-MG\", yes substring or equality works (base_key = atletico). If query is \"Athletico-PR\" -> canonical query = atleticopr, matches exactly.\n- Search stored canonicals for equality; if none, substring: stored canonical contains query_key OR query_key contains stored canonical? To handle query full name vs stored short name.\n- But avoid matching query \"Flamengo\" to \"Flamengo\" obviously.\n\nMaybe more robust: compute a `match_score(team_name, query)` using canonical keys and original names. But simple works.\n\nPotential issue: If query \"São Paulo\" canonical = 'saopaulo', stored 'saopaulofc' contains query -> match. Good. But stored 'saopaulo' can appear. Good.\nQuery \"Vasco\" canonical 'vasco', stored 'vascodagama' contains query. Good.\n\nBut query \"Bahia\" canonical 'bahia' may match many team names containing 'bahia' (e.g., 'Esporte Clube Bahia' canonical 'esporteclubebahia'); that's fine.\n\nLet me implement canonicalization carefully.\n\nNow query functions:\n\n`find_matches(team1=None, team2=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, limit=20)`:\n- team params can be canonicalized via `find_matching_canonicals(team, teams_catalog)`. The catalog can be generated once from all matches and players? Actually from matches. We'll build set of canonical team keys from matches only.\n- Filter matches. If both teams: match where (home_canonical in key1 and away_canonical in key2) OR (home_canonical in key2 and away_canonical in key1) if team1 vs team2 without order? spec says \"matches between two teams\" orderless. But for home/away may matter? We'll default to orderless for find matches between teams. If team1 only: either home or away in key1.\n- competition filter canonical name. Accept aliases:\n  - 'brasileirao','brasileirão','serie a','campeonato brasileiro' -> 'Brasileirão'\n  - 'copa do brasil','brazilian cup' -> 'Copa do Brasil'\n  - 'libertadores','copa libertadores' -> 'Copa Libertadores'\n- season filter: int.\n- date_from/to parse strings via `parse_date_param`.\n- Sort by date descending (most recent first), then limit. If no date, maybe season descending then round.\n- Return text with bullets: `date: home X-Y away (Competition Round/Stage)`.\n\n`team_statistics(team, season=None, competition=None)`:\n- Get matches involving team (home/away) using canonical match.\n- Wins: matches where team goals > opponent; Draws: equal; Losses: less.\n- Goals for/against.\n- Home record: filter home matches.\n- Away record: filter away matches.\n- Overall and optionally split.\n- Return formatted.\n\n`head_to_head(team1, team2)`:\n- Find matches between team1 and team2. Count wins, draws, losses. Return list + summary.\n\n`find_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, limit=20)`:\n- Filter players. Nationality: normalize accent-insensitive: if value contains 'brazil' or 'brasil' -> match 'Brazil','Brasil' etc. Use substring or canonical.\n- Club: match club name substring accent-insensitive. Use canonical matching on club? FIFA clubs have full names; can substring compare.\n- Position: exact/contains case-insensitive.\n- Sort by Overall desc then Name. Limit.\n- Return text.\n\n`top_players(nationality=None, club=None, position=None, limit=10)`:\n- Same as find_players but sorted by Overall desc, maybe no name filter. Limit.\n\n`competition_standings(competition, season)`:\n- Filter matches by competition and season.\n- For Brasileirão / league-like, compute points (3/1/0) per team.\n- Build dict team -> {'P','W','D','L','GF','GA','GD','Pts'}.\n- Sort by Pts descending, GD descending, GF descending, Wins descending, team name ascending.\n- Return ranked list.\n- If 'relegated_teams(season)': compute Brasileirão standings, return bottom 4.\n\n`biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)`:\n- Filter matches where both scores present. Compute abs diff. Sort descending. If ties sort by total goals? Use diff descending then date desc. Exclude matches with huge diff? Return.\n\n`average_goals(competition=None, season=None)`:\n- Compute mean total goals over matches. Return.\n\n`team_competitions(team)`:\n- Return list of competition/season pairs team played in.\n\n`get_player(name)`:\n- Find exact or fuzzy player name; return details.\n\nMCP server:\n- Use FastMCP with tools decorated functions. FastMCP handles stdio automatically if run `mcp run` or `python -m soccer_mcp.server`. For `if __name__ == '__main__'`, call `mcp.run(transport='stdio')`.\n- Define tools with type hints and docstrings; FastMCP can derive JSON schema from signatures. For complex types maybe use `str` and `int`. That's fine.\n\nData loading:\n- Since data loading can take a few seconds with 40k+ rows, load lazily at import. Maybe in server module, build engine at startup. Aggregate queries need all data, so load once. ~40k rows with csv.DictReader should be <1 sec.\n- In tests, to avoid loading full data each time (could be slow but OK), we can load once at module level.\n\nNow tests plan:\n- Use pytest. Maybe a fixture that builds engine once in `conftest.py`.\n- Test simple lookups:\n  - find matches Flamengo vs Fluminense returns matches with dates and scores.\n  - find matches Palmeiras in 2023.\n  - Copa do Brasil finals (round 'final' maybe? Need inspect round values).\n  - team stats Corinthians 2022.\n  - top Brazilian players.\n  - Flamengo players.\n  - fwd from São Paulo FC.\n  - 2019 Brasileirão standings contain Flamengo champion.\n  - 2020 relegated teams.\n  - avg goals etc.\n- Use BDD style comments.\n\nNeed inspect round fields for final naming.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_efccd840b001H7WJ2chMtdq4CP","sessionID":"ses_103385283ffe30G1XfRK45Z652","messageID":"msg_efccae8c8001VfxmfvBE6RRpMk"}}
