{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1782357978614,"sessionID":"ses_103385283ffe30G1XfRK45Z652","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"functions.bash:16","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport csv, os, re\nbase = '/private/var/folders/t6/p1v727892_qc9xqqdzq2rpym0000gn/T/retort-local-tjpsd7_l/retort-8ea0ffcc10d3/data/kaggle'\n\ndef canon(n):\n    n=n.strip()\n    n=re.sub(r'\\s*-\\s*[A-Z]{2}$','',n)\n    n=re.sub(r'\\s*\\([^)]*\\)','',n).strip()\n    return re.sub(r'[^A-Za-z0-9]+','',n).lower()\n\nfor fn in ['Brasileirao_Matches.csv','Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv','Libertadores_Matches.csv','BR-Football-Dataset.csv','novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv']:\n    c=0\n    with open(os.path.join(base,fn),encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        for r in csv.DictReader(f):\n            home=r.get('home_team') or r.get('home') or r.get('Equipe_mandante'); away=r.get('away_team') or r.get('away') or r.get('Equipe_visitante')\n            if {canon(home),canon(away)} == {'flamengo','fluminense'}:\n                c+=1\n    print(fn, c)\nPY","description":"Check Flamengo vs Fluminense match counts"},"output":"Brasileirao_Matches.csv 22\nBrazilian_Cup_Matches.csv 0\nLibertadores_Matches.csv 0\nBR-Football-Dataset.csv 21\nnovo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv 34\n","metadata":{"output":"Brasileirao_Matches.csv 22\nBrazilian_Cup_Matches.csv 0\nLibertadores_Matches.csv 0\nBR-Football-Dataset.csv 21\nnovo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv 34\n","exit":0,"description":"Check Flamengo vs Fluminense match counts","truncated":false},"title":"Check Flamengo vs Fluminense match counts","time":{"start":1782357978608,"end":1782357978612}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"         Copo do Brasil rounds are numeric, no explicit 'final'. So \"Find all Copa do Brasil finals\" perhaps means last round: round 8 (two-legged final maybe). We'll implement a function to infer final as max round for season. But our find_matches can accept round parameter? We'll add `round_number` param.\n\nNow implement. Need decide on module/package names. I will create package `brazilian_soccer_mcp` to avoid generic name. But tests import from it.\n\nLet me start writing files.\n\nFirst file: `pyproject.toml`.\n\nThen `brazilian_soccer_mcp/__init__.py`.\n\nThen `brazilian_soccer_mcp/normalize.py`.\n\nThen `brazilian_soccer_mcp/data_loader.py`.\n\nThen `brazilian_soccer_mcp/engine.py`.\n\nThen `brazilian_soccer_mcp/server.py`.\n\nThen `tests/conftest.py`, `tests/test_engine.py`.\n\nNeed ensure Block comments at top of files.\n\nLet's also update README? Since original prompt says update README. The user instruction didn't but implementing everything spec asks for doesn't include README update. However \"Finally update README.md\" in the ignored file. I'll skip unless necessary; maybe add small usage note. Could add section in README to describe code. But user didn't request. I might decide to update README after everything else if time.\n\nKey implementations:\n\n### normalize.py\n\n```python\nimport re\nimport unicodedata\n\nSTATE_SUFFIX_RE = re.compile(r'\\s*-\\s*([A-Z]{2})$')\nPARENS_RE = re.compile(r'\\s*\\([^)]*\\)')\nNON_ALNUM_RE = re.compile(r'[^a-z0-9]+')\n\n# Canonical aliases applied after stripping state suffix and before building the key.\n# Maps regex pattern -> replacement inside base string (after punctuation removed).\nALIASES = [\n    (re.compile(r'^athletico'), 'atletico'),  # Athletico Paranaense <-> Atlético-PR\n]\n\n# Common display aliases for queries where the user types a nickname or shorthand.\nDISPLAY_ALIASES = {\n    'spfc': 'São Paulo',\n    'timao': 'Corinthians',\n    'mengao': 'Flamengo',\n}\n\ndef strip_accents(text: str) -> str:\n    return ''.join(c for c in unicodedata.normalize('NFKD', text) if unicodedata.category(c) != 'Mn')\n\ndef clean_base(name: str):\n    \"\"\"Return (base_key, state_code).\"\"\"\n    name = name.strip()\n    # state suffix\n    m = STATE_SUFFIX_RE.search(name)\n    state = ''\n    if m:\n        state = m.group(1).upper()\n        name = name[:m.start()].strip()\n    # parentheses\n    name = PARENS_RE.sub('', name).strip()\n    name = re.sub(r'\\s+', ' ', name)\n    base = name.lower()\n    base = strip_accents(base)\n    base = NON_ALNUM_RE.sub('', base)\n    # aliases\n    for pat, repl in ALIASES:\n        base = pat.sub(repl, base)\n    return base, state\n\ndef canonical_team_name(name: str):\n    base, state = clean_base(name)\n    return (base + state).strip(), state, base\n\ndef competition_canonical(name: str):\n    n = name.lower()\n    n = strip_accents(n)\n    if 'libertadores' in n:\n        return 'Copa Libertadores'\n    if 'copa do brasil' in n or 'brazilian cup' in n:\n        return 'Copa do Brasil'\n    if 'brasileir' in n or 'campeonato brasileiro' in n or n == 'serie a' or n == 'seria a':\n        return 'Brasileirão'\n    return name.strip()\n\ndef parse_season(val):\n    if val is None or val == '':\n        return None\n    try:\n        return int(float(val))\n    except Exception:\n        return None\n\ndef parse_score(val):\n    if val is None or val == '':\n        return None\n    try:\n        return int(float(val))\n    except Exception:\n        return None\n\ndef parse_date(val):\n    \"\"\"Return datetime.date or None.\"\"\"\n    if val is None or val == '':\n        return None\n    from datetime import datetime\n    val = val.strip()\n    # remove time part after space if date present first\n    for fmt in ('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S', '%Y-%m-%d', '%d/%m/%Y'):\n        try:\n            return datetime.strptime(val, fmt).date()\n        except ValueError:\n            continue\n    # try with time component after date in dd/mm/yyyy\n    for fmt in ('%d/%m/%Y %H:%M:%S', '%d/%m/%Y %H:%M'):\n        try:\n            return datetime.strptime(val, fmt).date()\n        except ValueError:\n            continue\n    return None\n\ndef parse_date_param(val):\n    if isinstance(val, date):\n        return val\n    return parse_date(val)\n```\n\nNeed import date in parse_date_param; perhaps use datetime.date.\n\n### data_loader.py\n\nLoading all files. Need make relative paths work. Use `Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2] / 'data' / 'kaggle'`. But if server run from elsewhere, use that base. Since tests run from project root, relative okay.\n\n```python\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport csv\nfrom .normalize import (canonical_team_name, parse_season, parse_score, parse_date, competition_canonical)\n\nDATA_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2] / 'data' / 'kaggle'\n\ndef load_all():\n    matches = []\n    matches.extend(load_brasileirao())\n    ...\n    players = load_players()\n    return {\n        'matches': matches,\n        'players': players,\n        '_team_index': build_team_index(matches),\n    }\n\ndef load_csv(filename):\n    path = DATA_DIR / filename\n    with path.open(encoding='utf-8', newline='') as f:\n        return list(csv.DictReader(f))\n```\n\nDefine helper to add match.\n\nFor each file:\n\n- `Brasileirao_Matches.csv`:\n  rows with `home_team` and `away_team` have state suffix. We'll set display_name = original after stripping state suffix and parentheses. Use `canonical_team_name`. state from suffix. Round may be int.string; store as str.\n  \n- `Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv`: similar. Round from round. Some names include long full names and state suffix with space. Function handles. Stage None.\n  \n- `Libertadores_Matches.csv`: competition \"Copa Libertadores\". The season is float? Some may be string. parse.\n  \n- `BR-Football-Dataset.csv`: tournament determine competition canonical. home/away columns. date parse. round absent stage absent. Extended stats dict.\n  Note: this dataset has matches from Copa do Brasil and Brasileirão perhaps, duplicating other files. It's fine, include them. It may also include matches not relevant? All are presumably Brazilian football competitions or Brazilian teams.\n  \n- `novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv`: competition 'Brasileirão'. Date parse from `Data`. Round from `Rodada`. Arena from `Arena`. Use `Equipe_mandante`/`Equipe_visitante`.\n\nFor display name maybe create a function `display_name(name)` that strips state suffix and parenthetical content. Use original clean.\n\nPlayers:\n\n- `fifa_data.csv`: Load all columns as dict. For query, use `Name`, `Nationality`, `Club`, `Position`, `Overall`, etc. We may store original row, plus normalized fields:\n  - `name_normalized`: canonical name key? Not needed maybe.\n  - `nationality_normalized`: lower accent removed.\n  - `club_normalized`: canonical club name? We can apply similar matching later.\n\nUse `int` for Overall if present. Some columns may be missing or NaN? CSV strings; blank for missing. `Overall` appears floats maybe integer. parse_score works.\n\nFor player height/weight in strings; return as strings.\n\nBuild team index: set of all team canonical keys from matches.\n\nAlso build display map: canonical -> most common display name. To show friendly names.\n\n### engine.py\n\nUse a class `SoccerEngine`:\n\n```python\nclass SoccerEngine:\n    def __init__(self, matches, players):\n        self.matches = matches\n        self.players = players\n        self._team_index = self._build_team_index()\n```\n\nMethods:\n- `_matching_teams(name)` returns set of canonical keys matching query. Use canonical_team_name to get base/state. Implement matching:\n  - Compute query canonical `qkey` and base `qbase`.\n  - If qkey in team_index, return {qkey}.\n  - Else for each stored key, if qkey in stored_key or stored_key in qkey, add. But also if qbase in base part etc.\n  - Maybe also handle display aliases: if query base alnum in DISPLAY_ALIASES, add canonical of DISPLAY_ALIASES[name]? For \"SPFC\" -> canonical of \"São Paulo\". Then match stored key contains 'saopaulo'.\n  - Also map common query forms to a set of known canonicals? Hard.\n\nBut for engine we can use simple. However some tests will expect matching \"Corinthians\" across all variants. Our canonicalization will produce for \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" (if appears) -> 'sportclubcorinthianspaulista', contains 'corinthians'. Good. For raw \"Corinthians\" in data canonical 'corinthians'. So full result.\n\nPotential issue with `Brasileirao_Matches.csv` team \"Corinthians-SP\" canonical key = 'corinthianssp'? Because we append state code after base. For query \"Corinthians\" qkey='corinthians'. Stored key 'corinthianssp' contains qkey -> match. Good. For query \"Corinthians-SP\" qkey='corinthianssp', exact match.\n\nPotential issue with \"Vasco\" vs \"Vasco da Gama\". Data uses 'Vasco' maybe, and full names? canonical key 'vasco' or 'vascodagama', both match.\n\nNow implementing filters.\n\n`find_matches(...)`:\n- params: team1, team2, competition, season, date_from, date_to, round, stage, limit.\n- compute team1_keys, team2_keys.\n- For each match: check filters.\n- date_from/to using parse_date_param.\n- competition using competition_canonical.\n- round param can be int/string; compare match['round'] str(round).\n- stage param substring case-insensitive.\n- Sort by date descending (None dates last). We'll use min possible date for None? Put them last.\n- Format result text.\n\n`team_statistics(team, season=None, competition=None)`:\n- keys = matching team.\n- Filter matches where home_canonical in keys or away_canonical in keys. Also optional season/competition.\n- Compute overall, home, away stats. Use helper `team_stats_for(matches, keys)`.\n- If no season/competition and lots of matches, perhaps limit? Return all.\n- Format.\n\n`head_to_head(team1, team2, competition=None, season=None, limit=20)`:\n- keys1, keys2. Filter matches where home in keys1 and away in keys2 OR home in keys2 and away in keys1.\n- Determine perspective for summary: wins/losses/draws for team1. For each match, decide team1 goals. If team1 home, use home. Else away.\n- Return sorted by date desc.\n\n`find_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, limit=20, top=False)`:\n- Filter. If name, canonicalize? Could search substring in normalized name. Use simple: lower name and search.\n- nationality: normalize; if query contains 'brazil'/'brasil', accept either; else substring in nationality normalized.\n- club: use team matching on club names. Since clubs in FIFA may be international not just Brazilian, canonicalize and substring.\n- position: case-insensitive full or substring? We'll use starts-with/contains if query short.\n- Sort by Overall desc, Name asc. Convert Overall to int if possible; treat missing as 0.\n- Limit.\n\n`top_players(...)` use find_players with top=True? Actually top players might include name filter too. We'll reuse.\n\n`competition_standings(competition, season)`:\n- Filter matches by competition canonical and season.\n- Use normalized team canonical as team key; display name from display map.\n- Points: win 3, draw 1.\n- Sort.\n- Return lines with rank.\n\n`relegated_teams(season)`: compute Brasileirão standings for season, return bottom 4.\n\n`biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)`:\n- Filter matches with both scores not None. diff = abs(home_goal-away_goal). sort desc.\n- Return formatted.\n\n`average_goals(competition=None, season=None)`:\n- Compute average total goals per match (including None as 0? exclude matches without scores). Return.\n\n`player_details(name)`: find exact/fuzzy.\n\n### server.py\n\nUse FastMCP:\n```python\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nfrom .data_loader import load_all\nfrom .engine import SoccerEngine\n\nmcp = FastMCP('brazilian_soccer_mcp')\n\nDATA = load_all()\nENGINE = SoccerEngine(DATA['matches'], DATA['players'])\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef find_matches(...) -> str:\n   ...\n```\n\nTool functions return str. Include type hints for schema.\n\nNeed make server import fast. The load_all at import may be ~1 second. Fine.\n\nNeed entry point: `if __name__ == '__main__': mcp.run(transport='stdio')`\n\nAdd `__main__.py` in package or run `python -m brazilian_soccer_mcp.server`.\n\nAlso create a `main.py` top-level for convenience that imports server.mcp.run. But main.py file also a code file; acceptable.\n\n### tests\n\n`tests/conftest.py`:\n```python\nimport pytest\nfrom brazilian_soccer_mcp.data_loader import load_all\nfrom brazilian_soccer_mcp.engine import SoccerEngine\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope='session')\ndef engine():\n    data = load_all()\n    return SoccerEngine(data['matches'], data['players'])\n```\n\nTests should be BDD style. Use comments like:\n```python\ndef test_find_flamengo_fluminense_matches(engine):\n    # Given the match data is loaded\n    # When I search for matches between Flamengo and Fluminense\n    result = engine.head_to_head('Flamengo', 'Fluminense')\n    # Then I should receive a list of matches with dates, scores, and competition\n    assert 'Flamengo' in result\n    ...\n```\n\nBut we need actual data to test with: does Flamengo vs Fluminense appear in Brasileirao_Matches? Let me check quickly.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_efccf8370001RtyhilO4gXSQrh","sessionID":"ses_103385283ffe30G1XfRK45Z652","messageID":"msg_efccda2950011AZbV13wXRKZgX"}}
