{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1782358643415,"sessionID":"ses_103385283ffe30G1XfRK45Z652","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"functions.bash:38","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport csv, os, collections\nbase = '/private/var/folders/t6/p1v727892_qc9xqqdzq2rpym0000gn/T/retort-local-tjpsd7_l/retort-8ea0ffcc10d3/data/kaggle'\nteams=set()\nfor fn in ['Brasileirao_Matches.csv','novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv','BR-Football-Dataset.csv']:\n    with open(os.path.join(base,fn),encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        for r in csv.DictReader(f):\n            home=r.get('home_team') or r.get('home') or r.get('Equipe_mandante')\n            away=r.get('away_team') or r.get('away') or r.get('Equipe_visitante')\n            for t in (home,away):\n                low=t.lower()\n                if 'atlético' in low or 'atletico' in low or 'athletico' in low or 'athetico' in low:\n                    teams.add(t)\nprint(sorted(teams)[:30])\nprint('total distinct Atletico names', len(teams))\nPY","description":"Inspect Atlético team name variants"},"output":"['Athletico Paranaense', 'Athletico-PR', 'Atletico Acreano', 'Atletico Alagoinhas', 'Atletico Goianiense', 'Atletico Mineiro', 'Atletico Paranaense', 'Atletico-GO', 'Atletico-MG', 'Atletico-PR', 'Atlético-GO', 'Atlético-MG', 'FC Atlético Cearense']\ntotal distinct Atletico names 13\n","metadata":{"output":"['Athletico Paranaense', 'Athletico-PR', 'Atletico Acreano', 'Atletico Alagoinhas', 'Atletico Goianiense', 'Atletico Mineiro', 'Atletico Paranaense', 'Atletico-GO', 'Atletico-MG', 'Atletico-PR', 'Atlético-GO', 'Atlético-MG', 'FC Atlético Cearense']\ntotal distinct Atletico names 13\n","exit":0,"description":"Inspect Atlético team name variants","truncated":false},"title":"Inspect Atlético team name variants","time":{"start":1782358643411,"end":1782358643413}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"                           Uh oh, we have both `flamengo` and `flamengoRJ` canonical keys, `atleticoPR` and `atleticoMG`, etc. This means state suffix extraction is not working for some data sources. In `novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv`, state columns are separate `Mandante_UF` and `Visitante_UF`, not appended to team names, so canonical for \"Flamengo\" is 'flamengo' (no state). In `Brasileirao_Matches.csv`, names include `-RJ`, so canonical includes state 'flamengoRJ'. They are not merged. We need normalize: if a data source has state suffix, strip it to match sources without suffix. But earlier I designed canonical to include state suffix to distinguish Atlético-MG from Atlético-PR. However majority of data sources include state suffix for one group and not for others. To match all, maybe we should strip state suffix entirely from canonical and rely on full names to differentiate. But that would collapse Atlético-MG and Atlético-PR into one `atletico`, which breaks two distinct teams. Is there another way? We can append state suffix only sometimes? This is tricky.\n\nObservations:\n- `Brasileirao_Matches.csv` uses suffix for all teams: e.g., \"Flamengo-RJ\", \"Palmeiras-SP\".\n- `novo_campeonato_brasileiro` uses no suffix, separate state columns.\n- `BR-Football-Dataset` uses no suffix (or maybe some with suffix? sample shows \"São Paulo\", \"Flamengo\", \"Cruzeiro\", etc., no suffix).\n- `Brazilian_Cup_Matches` sometimes includes suffix? first row \"Boavista Sport Club ... - RJ\", \"América - MG\". Yes includes suffix sometimes.\n- `Libertadores` no suffix mostly, includes \"(URU)\" etc.\n\nIf we strip state suffix from canonical, Corinthians becomes one for all. Atlético-MG and Atlético-PR must be distinguished differently. Raw names: \"Atlético-MG\" vs \"Atlético-PR\" -> after stripping suffix both base \"atletico\" + state removed. Collapse. However full names of these clubs in different datasets: \"Atlético Mineiro\" vs \"Athletico Paranaense\" / \"Atlético Paranaense\". Without state suffix, we can distinguish by base name words 'mineiro' vs 'paranaense' if present. For `Brasileirao_Matches.csv` names are \"Atlético-MG\" and \"Atlético-PR\" (or \"Athletico-PR\" with alias). Strips suffix -> base \"atletico\". No 'mineiro'/'paranaense' in name. So cannot distinguish. We could map \"Atletico-MG\" to base \"atleticomineiro\", \"Atletico-PR\" to \"atleticoparanaense\" using state code but only if state suffix was present. Then canonical would differ. But for `novo` data, \"Atlético Mineiro\" canonical \"atleticomineiro\" (because word mineiro included), and \"Atlético Paranaense\" canonical \"atleticoparanaense\". These would match. For `Brasileirao_Matches`, we would map \"-MG\" to suffix 'mg', and append only for Minas? But the state alone wouldn't tell us which base to add. We can append state code only when base is too generic? Hmm.\n\nBetter: create a normalisation that maps state abbreviations to region descriptors for known ambiguous teams. But maybe we can accept collapsing Atlético teams? In 2019, Atlético-MG and Atlético-PR would merge; points combined would be high (119! That matches), and it became top. So not acceptable.\n\nAlternative: Append state suffix only if the base is generic (common) and state not already part of base. We can detect generic base names: if base in a set of generic/ambiguous names like {'atletico', 'athletico', 'sport', 'america'}? Then append state code. For non-generic names like Flamengo, Palmeiras, we strip state suffix to unify. But also \"Corinthians\" is not generic, can strip. \"Sport\" is generic (could be Sport Recife vs other Sports). \"América\" generic: América-MG vs América-RN. \"Fortaleza\"? Could be Fortaleza CE vs other? Not likely. \"Cruzeiro\"? unique. \"Vasco\"? unique. So rule: strip state suffix for canonical EXCEPT when the base part is in an AMBIGUOUS_BASES set. For ambiguous names, append state to canonical.\n\nLet's define AMBIGUOUS_BASES = {'atletico', 'athletico', 'sport', 'america', 'nacional'}? 'Nacional' appears in Libertadores as \"Nacional (URU)\" or \"Nacional\" (different countries). Keep 'nacional' ambiguous. Also 'santos' no. 'internacional' maybe unique. 'flamengo' unique. 'palmeiras' unique. 'corinthians' unique. 'saopaulo' unique. 'gremio' unique. 'botafogo' maybe Botafogo RJ vs Botafogo PB; ambiguous? Could include 'botafogo'. 'cuiaba' unique. 'ceara' unique. 'bahia' unique. 'goias' maybe. Let's add 'botafogo' ambiguous. 'vitoria' ambiguous? Vitória-BA vs Vitória (other). Add 'vitoria'. 'santa' not full.\n\nThus canonical: if base in AMBIGUOUS_BASES, key = base + state code; else key = base (strip suffix always). Then query matching using canonical. This will unify Flamengo/RJ and Flamengo, and differentiate Atlético teams.\n\nNeed adjust team name normalization accordingly. The `canonical_team_name` currently returns base+state. Change to optional state. Update `_clean_base`? Actually canonical returns base (or base+state for ambiguous). Keep state_code separate for display maybe.\n\nBut what about team queries like \"Corinthians\" should match \"Corinthians-SP\" (base 'corinthians', no state in key? Actually canonical for Corinthians-SP with new rule base='corinthians' not ambiguous -> key 'corinthians'. Good.\n\"Flamengo-RJ\" canonical 'flamengo'. Query 'Flamengo' key 'flamengo'.\n\"Atlético-MG\" base 'atletico', ambiguous -> key 'atleticomg'. \"Atlético Mineiro\" canonical 'atleticomineiro'? Wait base = 'atleticomineiro' because word mineiro not removed. That's not equal to 'atleticomg'. Need map for full names: if base contains 'mineiro' or 'paranaense' etc. We can normalize base for Atlético: if base starts with 'atletico' and ends with state descriptor? Could map 'atleticomineiro' -> 'atleticomg', 'atleticoparanaense' -> 'atleticopr'. Or when ambiguous and base includes 'mg'? In `Brasileirao_Matches`, 'Atletico-MG' -> state code 'MG', base 'atletico' -> key 'atleticomg'. In `novo`, 'Atlético Mineiro' -> state column 'MG' not in name, base 'atleticomineiro' -> ambiguous? It includes 'mineiro', maybe we need map to 'atleticomg' based on descriptor. Similarly 'Athletico Paranaense' -> descriptor 'paranaense' -> state PR. It might also be 'Atletico Paranaense'. So define a descriptor to state mapping: mineiro -> MG, paranaense -> PR, paulista -> SP? But 'Corinthians Paulista' maybe? Could ignore. For ambiguous bases, maybe also use descriptor instead of raw state. Alternatively, for these teams treat canonical as base + state if available, and also generate alias canonical base+descriptor. Complex.\n\nSimpler: Use a fuller alias table for common Brazilian clubs mapping parenthetical/long forms to short canonical keys with state. Example:\n- \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" -> \"corinthians\"\n- \"Clube de Regatas do Flamengo\" -> \"flamengo\"\n- \"Sociedade Esportiva Palmeiras\" -> \"palmeiras\"\n- \"São Paulo Futebol Clube\" -> \"saopaulo\"\n- \"Santos Futebol Clube\" -> \"santos\"\n- \"Clube Atlético Mineiro\" / \"Atlético Mineiro\" / \"Atlético-MG\" -> \"atleticomg\"\n- \"Clube Athletico Paranaense\" / \"Athletico Paranaense\" / \"Atlético Paranaense\" / \"Athletico-PR\" / \"Atlético-PR\" -> \"atleticopr\"\n- \"Sport Club Internacional\" -> \"internacional\"\n- \"Grêmio Foot-Ball Porto Alegrense\" -> \"gremio\"\n- \"Futebol Clube do Porto\"? not relevant.\n- \"Sport Club do Recife\" / \"Sport\" -> \"sportrecife\"? Actually Sport Club do Recife commonly \"Sport\". But state PE. Could map to \"sport\".\n\nBut we don't know whether full names appear. Data mostly uses short names with state suffix. The main issue is Atlético and América and Nacional. We can handle by using regex canonical: after stripping state suffix, if base contains a known state descriptor, replace with state code for ambiguous roots. Something like:\n```\nAMBIGUOUS = {'atletico', 'athletico', 'sport', 'america', 'nacional', 'botafogo', 'vitoria'}\nDESCRIPTOR_STATE = {'mineiro':'mg', 'paranaense':'pr', 'baiano':'ba', 'paulista':'sp', 'potiguar':'rn', 'cearense':'ce', 'fluminense':'rj', ...}\n```\n\nFor ambiguous root:\n- descriptor = any word in DESCRIPTOR_STATE found in base after root.\n- state = either explicit state suffix from name or inferred descriptor.\n- canonical = root + state.\n- also keep non-ambiguous names with root? no.\n\nBut for \"Botafogo-RJ\" root 'botafogo', ambiguous, explicit state 'RJ' -> botafogorj. \"Botafogo-PB\" -> botafogopb. Good. For \"Sport\" root 'sport'; if no descriptor in base and state suffix present (e.g., Sport-PE from `Brasileirao_Matches`?), use state. For \"América-MG\" root 'america' state mg -> americamg. Good.\n\nBut for \"Atlético Mineiro\" from novo: root 'atletico', descriptor 'mineiro' -> state mg, canonical 'atleticomg'. Good. For \"Athletico Paranaense\" root 'atletico' (alias applied), descriptor 'paranaense' -> state pr -> 'atleticopr'. Good. For \"Atlético-PR\" root 'atletico', state pr explicit -> 'atleticopr'. Good.\n\nBut wait, `Brasileirao_Matches` has \"Atlético-MG\"? Let's check.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_efcda827c001AjHFRT0012B0Kj","sessionID":"ses_103385283ffe30G1XfRK45Z652","messageID":"msg_efcd9aa15001q3f9eSwUeP2Gm6"}}
