{"type":"text","timestamp":1782359539997,"sessionID":"ses_103385283ffe30G1XfRK45Z652","part":{"id":"prt_efce7ae5f001hQKZPCoBuCTk7L","messageID":"msg_efce7a203001h8wf1DiT6uNv7C","sessionID":"ses_103385283ffe30G1XfRK45Z652","type":"text","text":" Done. I implemented the Brazilian Soccer MCP server as specified in `TASK.md` and verified it builds and all tests pass.\n\nWhat was created\n\n- `pyproject.toml` – Python package config with `mcp` dependency and pytest dev extras\n- `brazilian_soccer_mcp/`\n  - `__init__.py` – package/version info\n  - `normalize.py` – team-name, competition, date and score normalisation\n  - `data_loader.py` – loads all 6 CSVs and de-duplicates overlapping league fixtures\n  - `engine.py` – query engine for matches, teams, players, competitions and statistics\n  - `server.py` – MCP stdio server exposing the engine as tools via `mcp.server.fastmcp`\n  - `__main__.py` – module entry point\n- `main.py` – convenience entry point\n- `tests/`\n  - `conftest.py` – session-scoped fixtures\n  - `test_engine.py` – BDD-style pytest scenarios covering matches, teams, players, competitions, stats, normalisation and data coverage\n- `.gitignore` – ignores `venv/`, `__pycache__/`, build artifacts\n- `README.md` – added usage and test instructions\n\nHow to use\n\n```bash\n# Run the MCP server\npython -m brazilian_soccer_mcp\n# or\npython main.py\n\n# Run tests\npython -m pytest tests -q\n```\n\nInstall/build verification\n\n```bash\npython -m venv venv\nvenv/bin/pip install -e '.[dev]'\nvenv/bin/pytest tests -q\n```\n\nResult: 26 passed in both the system Python environment and a fresh editable-install venv. The server also responded correctly to an MCP `initialize` request over stdio.","time":{"start":1782359502431,"end":1782359539991}}}
